                                                    "bodypart": "Bones",
                                                    "iscontrast": "yes",
                                                    "procedureType": null,
                                                    "recoveryTime": null,
                                                    "preparationInstructions": "<p>A small amount of radioactive tracer is injected into a vein in the arm. The tracer circulates through the bloodstream and accumulates in bones. The patient is asked to wait for a few hours to allow proper distribution. During this time, normal activities may be permitted. The patient is then positioned on a scanning table. A special gamma camera captures images of the skeletal system. The patient must remain still during the scan. The procedure usually takes about 30 to 60 minutes. The process is painless and non-invasive. After completion, the patient can resume normal activities. The images are analyzed by a nuclear medicine specialist.</p>",
