                                                    "recoveryTime": null,
                                                    "preparationInstructions": "<p>A radioactive tracer is injected intravenously. The patient rests quietly during the uptake phase, usually 45–60 minutes. Minimal movement is essential during this period. The patient is then positioned on the PET CT scanning table. The scan begins with CT imaging followed by PET acquisition. The table moves slowly through the scanner capturing whole-body data. Imaging typically takes 20–30 minutes depending on protocol. The patient must remain still throughout acquisition. Data from PET and CT are fused to create combined images. Post-scan hydration is encouraged to aid tracer clearance. Normal activities can usually be resumed after completion. Images are analyzed by a nuclear medicine specialist.</p>",
