                                                    "recoveryTime": null,
                                                    "preparationInstructions": "<p>A radioactive tracer is injected into a vein. The patient is positioned under a gamma camera. Imaging begins immediately to track tracer uptake by kidneys. Sequential images are captured to assess blood flow and filtration. The tracer movement through ureters into bladder is monitored. The patient may be asked to void during the procedure. Additional images may be taken after bladder emptying. The scan provides time-based functional data of kidneys. The procedure typically lasts 30–60 minutes. Hydration may be encouraged to improve tracer clearance. Data is processed into functional graphs and images. A nuclear medicine specialist interprets the results.</p>",
