                                                    "procedureType": null,
                                                    "recoveryTime": null,
                                                    "preparationInstructions": "<p>A radioactive tracer is injected intravenously. The patient waits for a specific uptake period, usually a few hours. During this time, the tracer accumulates in active bone areas. The patient is positioned under a SPECT scanner. The camera rotates around the body to acquire 3D images. Multiple slices are reconstructed for detailed evaluation. The patient must remain still during acquisition. Imaging duration typically ranges from 20–30 minutes. Additional CT may be combined for better localization. Post-scan hydration is encouraged to clear tracer. Images are processed into cross-sectional views. A nuclear medicine specialist interprets the findings.</p>",
