                                                    "preparationInstructions": "<p>A radioactive tracer is injected into a vein. The patient is positioned under a gamma camera. Initial images capture baseline gland uptake. After some time, a stimulant (like lemon juice) is given. This triggers salivary secretion for dynamic imaging. Sequential images track tracer washout from glands. The scan records both uptake and excretion phases. The patient must remain still during imaging. The procedure typically takes around 30–45 minutes. Multiple time-based images are captured. Functional curves are generated for analysis. A nuclear medicine specialist interprets gland performance.</p>",
