                                                    "precaution": "<p>* Always store and use the oxygen cylinder in an upright and secure position. * Keep the cylinder away from open flames, smoking materials, sparks, and flammable substances. * Ensure proper ventilation in the area where the oxygen cylinder is being used or stored. * Use only approved oxygen regulators, flow meters, and compatible accessories. * Check the cylinder valve, regulator, pressure gauge, tubing, and connections regularly for leakage or damage. * Open the cylinder valve slowly and carefully to prevent sudden pressure release. * Avoid dropping, dragging, or striking the oxygen cylinder during transportation or handling. * Secure the cylinder properly using a stand, trolley, or support chain to prevent falling. * Keep oil, grease, and petroleum-based products away from the cylinder valve and fittings. * Do not expose the oxygen cylinder to excessive heat, direct sunlight, or high temperatures. * Ensure oxygen tubing remains free from bends, twists, or obstruction during use. * Use the oxygen cylinder only according to the prescribed oxygen flow settings advised by healthcare professionals. * Do not use the cylinder if unusual leakage, damage, rust, or valve malfunction is noticed. * Elderly or critically ill patients should use oxygen therapy under caregiver or medical supervision if necessary. * Store empty and filled cylinders separately in a clean, dry, and safe environment.</p>",
                                                    "sideeffects": "<p>* Keep the oxygen cylinder away from open flames, smoking materials, sparks, and flammable substances at all times. * Always store and operate the cylinder in an upright and secure position. * Ensure the oxygen cylinder is properly secured using a trolley, stand, or support chain to prevent falling. * Use only approved oxygen regulators, flow meters, and compatible medical accessories. * Inspect the cylinder valve, regulator, pressure gauge, tubing, and fittings regularly for leakage or damage. * Open the cylinder valve slowly and carefully to avoid sudden pressure release. * Keep oil, grease, petroleum products, and oily hands away from oxygen valves and fittings. * Do not expose the oxygen cylinder to excessive heat, direct sunlight, or high-temperature environments. * Ensure oxygen tubing remains free from twisting, bending, or obstruction during use. * Use the oxygen cylinder only with prescribed oxygen flow settings recommended by healthcare professionals. * Do not drag, drop, strike, or mishandle the cylinder during transportation or storage. * Replace damaged regulators, valves, tubing, or accessories immediately to maintain safety. * Elderly, critically ill, or oxygen-dependent patients should use oxygen therapy under caregiver or medical supervision if necessary. * Do not use the oxygen cylinder if unusual leakage, rust, damage, or valve malfunction is observed. * Store empty and filled cylinders separately in a clean, dry, well-ventilated, and safe environment.</p>",
