                                                    "_id": "69a13436a60e62715b1764e0",
                                                    "name": "Stroke Rehabilitation at Home",
                                                    "description": "<h4>Stroke Rehabilitation at Home is a specialized neurological rehabilitation and physiotherapy service designed to help stroke survivors recover physical function, mobility, balance, speech coordination, muscle strength, and independence within the comfort of their home environment. A stroke can significantly affect movement, coordination, muscle control, balance, communication, and daily activities, making professional rehabilitation essential for improving recovery outcomes and quality of life.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>This service is especially beneficial for patients recovering from ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke, paralysis, muscle weakness, balance disorders, speech coordination difficulties, reduced mobility, post-hospitalization weakness, swallowing difficulties, and neurological movement limitations. Elderly patients, bedridden individuals, and patients requiring long-term neurological rehabilitation can also benefit from continuous home-based recovery support.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Trained neurological physiotherapists, rehabilitation specialists, and healthcare professionals provide personalized therapy programs according to the patient’s stroke condition, mobility level, physical limitations, muscle strength, rehabilitation goals, and doctor’s recommendations. The treatment plan may include stretching exercises, muscle strengthening, balance training, gait training, posture correction, coordination exercises, mobility support, neurological rehabilitation techniques, breathing exercises, sensory stimulation activities, and functional movement training to improve independence and daily living skills.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Stroke Rehabilitation at Home also includes continuous monitoring for muscle stiffness, weakness, balance problems, pain, fatigue, reduced movement, swallowing difficulties, speech coordination issues, or rehabilitation-related complications. Early identification of concerns helps improve patient safety and supports more effective long-term neurological recovery.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Healthcare professionals educate patients and caregivers regarding safe movement techniques, fall prevention, posture support, walking aid usage, home exercise routines, positioning techniques, breathing exercises, nutritional support, and long-term stroke recovery management. Emotional encouragement and compassionate rehabilitation care also help improve patient confidence, motivation, and participation during recovery.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Receiving Stroke Rehabilitation at Home allows patients to continue recovery comfortably within a familiar and emotionally supportive environment while reducing travel-related stress and physical strain. The service promotes improved mobility, enhanced balance and coordination, better muscle control, increased physical independence, safer neurological recovery, improved daily functioning, and continuous professional rehabilitation support at home.</h4>",
