                                                    "strength": null,
                                                    "directionofuse": null,
                                                    "precaution": "<p>* Always ensure all wheel locks and brakes are securely engaged before patient transfer or positioning. * Check the frame, wheels, brakes, side rails, reclining mechanisms, and adjustment systems regularly for damage or looseness. * Do not exceed the recommended weight capacity of the wheelchair stretcher. * Ensure the patient is properly positioned and balanced before movement or reclining adjustments. * Use seat belts, safety straps, and side rails properly for additional patient safety. * Adjust reclining or stretcher conversion mechanisms slowly and carefully to avoid sudden movement. * Move the wheelchair stretcher slowly and cautiously, especially on ramps, uneven surfaces, elevators, or crowded areas. * Ensure footrests, armrests, and side supports are securely fixed before transportation. * Avoid sudden pushing, rapid turns, or rough handling during patient transfer. * Elderly, unconscious, critically ill, or physically weak patients should always be monitored during transportation. * Keep hands, fingers, loose clothing, and accessories away from moving or folding parts. * Clean and disinfect the mattress, seat, frame, and support surfaces regularly to maintain hygiene. * Do not use the wheelchair stretcher if any structural part becomes unstable, bent, cracked, or damaged. * Use proper patient handling techniques to reduce injury risk for both patients and caregivers. * Store the wheelchair stretcher in a clean, dry, and safe location when not in use.</p>",
                                                    "sideeffects": "<p>Always engage all wheel locks or brakes before transferring, positioning, or attending to the patient. * Ensure side rails, safety belts, footrests, and armrests are securely fixed before transportation. * Do not exceed the recommended weight capacity of the wheelchair stretcher. * Keep the patient properly positioned and balanced during movement or reclining adjustments. * Adjust reclining or stretcher conversion mechanisms slowly to avoid sudden imbalance or discomfort. * Move the wheelchair stretcher carefully on ramps, slopes, uneven floors, elevators, or wet surfaces. * Avoid sudden pushing, rapid turns, or rough handling during patient transportation. * Inspect the wheels, brakes, frame, side rails, mattress, and adjustment systems regularly for damage or looseness. * Replace worn-out wheels, damaged brakes, or defective components immediately to maintain safety. * Elderly, unconscious, critically ill, or physically weak patients should always be monitored during transportation. * Keep hands, fingers, loose clothing, and accessories away from moving or folding parts. * Use proper patient handling and transfer techniques to reduce injury risk for both patients and caregivers. * Clean and disinfect the mattress, frame, and support surfaces regularly to maintain hygiene and patient safety. * Do not use the wheelchair stretcher if it becomes unstable, bent, cracked, or structurally damaged. * Store the wheelchair stretcher in a clean, dry, and safe place away from excessive moisture or corrosive substances.</p>",
                                                    "complexity": null,
