                                                    "_id": "6986f2d971d1b4078acc4dd3",
                                                    "name": "Coronary Stent Placement",
                                                    "description": "<p>Coronary Stent Placement is a minimally invasive cardiac procedure performed to open blocked or narrowed coronary arteries.</p><p>The procedure helps restore proper blood flow to the heart muscles and reduces the risk of heart attack and severe cardiac complications.</p><p>It is commonly recommended for patients suffering from coronary artery disease, chest pain, or reduced blood circulation to the heart.</p><p>During the procedure, a thin catheter with a balloon is inserted through a blood vessel in the arm or groin and guided to the blocked artery.</p><p>A small mesh tube called a stent is placed inside the artery to keep it open permanently.</p><p>Drug-eluting stents or bare-metal stents may be used depending on the patient’s condition.</p><p>The procedure is usually performed under local anesthesia by experienced interventional cardiologists.</p><p>Diagnostic tests such as ECG, angiography, echocardiogram, blood tests, and cardiac evaluations are performed before treatment.</p><p>Coronary Stent Placement helps relieve chest pain, improves blood circulation, and enhances overall heart function.</p><p>Most patients experience faster recovery compared to open-heart surgical procedures.</p><p>Lifestyle modifications and medications are important after the procedure to maintain long-term heart health.</p><p>Regular follow-up consultations help monitor the condition of the stent and heart function.</p>",
