                                                    "strength": null,
                                                    "directionofuse": "<p>Patients should undergo angiography, ECG, blood tests, and cardiac evaluation before the procedure.</p><p>Doctors may advise temporary discontinuation of certain medications before treatment.</p><p>Patients should follow fasting instructions and maintain hydration before the procedure.</p><p>After stent placement, patients are monitored for several hours or overnight in the hospital.</p><p>Prescribed blood thinner medications should be taken regularly to prevent clot formation.</p><p>Patients should gradually resume normal physical activity based on medical advice.</p><p>A heart-healthy diet low in cholesterol and unhealthy fats is recommended after the procedure.</p><p>Smoking and alcohol should be avoided to maintain long-term heart health.</p><p>Regular monitoring of blood pressure, sugar levels, and cholesterol is important.</p><p>Routine follow-up visits help evaluate stent function and overall cardiac condition.</p><p>Light walking and cardiac rehabilitation may help improve recovery and heart function.</p><p>Immediate medical consultation is necessary if severe chest pain, swelling, or unusual symptoms develop.</p>",
                                                    "precaution": "<p>Patients should follow all instructions provided by the cardiologist before the procedure.</p><p>Smoking and alcohol consumption should be avoided before and after treatment.</p><p>Blood-thinning medications should only be taken as advised by the doctor.</p><p>Patients should inform the medical team about allergies, diabetes, kidney disease, or other existing conditions.</p><p>Fasting for several hours before the procedure may be required.</p><p>Proper hydration and infection prevention measures should be maintained.</p><p>Heavy physical activities and lifting should be avoided during the initial recovery period.</p><p>Patients should regularly monitor blood pressure, cholesterol, and blood sugar levels.</p><p>A heart-healthy lifestyle and balanced diet are strongly recommended.</p><p>Routine follow-up visits and medication adherence are essential after stent placement.</p><p>Immediate medical attention is necessary if chest pain or breathing difficulty occurs after the procedure.</p><p>Patients should not stop prescribed blood thinner medications without medical advice.</p>",
                                                    "sideeffects": "<p>Coronary Stent Placement is generally safe but may involve certain risks and complications.</p><p>Bleeding or bruising at the catheter insertion site may occur after the procedure.</p><p>Blood clot formation inside the stent is a possible complication.</p><p>Some patients may experience allergic reactions to contrast dye or medications.</p><p>Irregular heartbeat or temporary chest discomfort may occur after treatment.</p><p>Infection at the insertion site is rare but possible.</p><p>There is a small risk of artery damage or restenosis, where the artery narrows again over time.</p><p>Kidney complications may occur in patients sensitive to contrast dye.</p><p>Low blood pressure or dizziness may be experienced temporarily after the procedure.</p><p>Pain, swelling, or mild discomfort near the insertion area may occur during recovery.</p><p>Proper medication usage and follow-up care help reduce the risk of complications.</p><p>Patients should immediately report severe chest pain, bleeding, or breathing difficulty to the doctor.</p>",
                                                    "complexity": "simple",
