                                                    "_id": "699ec43798865815513a2b6b",
                                                    "name": "Spinal Fusion",
                                                    "description": "<h4>Spinal Fusion is a major spine surgery performed to permanently join two or more vertebrae (spinal bones) so they heal into a single solid bone. The procedure is commonly used to treat conditions such as degenerative disc disease, spinal instability, severe back or neck pain, scoliosis, spinal fractures, spondylolisthesis, and certain cases of nerve compression. The main goal is to stabilize the spine, reduce pain caused by movement between unstable vertebrae, and prevent further structural damage.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>The procedure is usually performed by a spine surgeon or neurosurgeon under general anesthesia in a hospital or specialized spine center. During surgery, the damaged disc between vertebrae is often removed, and bone grafts (from the patient or donor) or synthetic materials are placed between the vertebrae. Metal implants such as screws, rods, or plates are used to hold the bones in place while they fuse together over time.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Modern spinal fusion procedures are supported by advanced imaging systems, robotic-assisted surgical navigation, intraoperative monitoring, minimally invasive spine techniques, and precision implant systems that improve surgical accuracy, reduce complications, and support better alignment and stability outcomes.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Spinal fusion is highly beneficial for stabilizing the spine, reducing chronic pain, correcting deformity, relieving nerve compression, and improving functional movement and quality of life. It is often recommended when non-surgical treatments such as medications, physiotherapy, or injections fail to provide relief.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Like all major spine surgeries, spinal fusion carries certain risks including infection, bleeding, nerve injury, blood clots, implant failure, non-union (failure of bones to fuse), stiffness, reduced spinal flexibility, and anesthesia-related complications. Recovery may take several months as bone fusion is a gradual healing process.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Proper post-operative care, physiotherapy, posture correction, and lifestyle modification are essential for successful fusion and long-term spine health.</h4>",
                                                    "subcategory": "698b2b7caced83dadfe6b1e9",
                                                    "brand": null,
                                                    "composition": null,
                                                    "createdAt": "2026-02-25T09:43:19.054Z",
