                                                    "_id": "699ec8c798865815513a2c31",
                                                    "name": "Artificial Disc Replacement",
                                                    "description": "<h4>Artificial Disc Replacement is a specialized spine surgery performed to replace a damaged or degenerated intervertebral disc with an artificial implant. The procedure is commonly used to treat chronic neck or lower back pain caused by degenerative disc disease, disc herniation, spinal instability, or nerve compression when conservative treatments fail. The goal of the surgery is to relieve pain, maintain spinal motion, and restore normal spine function while preserving natural movement compared to spinal fusion.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>The procedure is typically performed by a spine surgeon or orthopedic/neurosurgeon under general anesthesia in a hospital or specialized spine center. During the surgery, the damaged disc is carefully removed and replaced with an artificial disc made of metal and medical-grade polymer materials designed to mimic natural disc movement. The implant helps maintain spinal flexibility while relieving pressure on nerves and surrounding structures.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Modern artificial disc replacement procedures are supported by advanced imaging systems, minimally invasive spine techniques, intraoperative navigation technology, motion-preserving implant systems, and multidisciplinary rehabilitation programs that improve surgical accuracy, reduce complications, and support faster recovery outcomes.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Artificial disc replacement is highly beneficial for reducing chronic spinal pain, improving neck or back mobility, restoring nerve function, maintaining spinal flexibility, and enhancing quality of life. It is often preferred over spinal fusion in selected patients because it preserves motion at the treated spinal level.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Like all major spine surgeries, artificial disc replacement carries certain risks including infection, bleeding, nerve injury, implant failure, persistent pain, stiffness, spinal fluid leakage, blood clots, and anesthesia-related complications. Rarely, revision surgery may be required if the implant shifts or does not function properly. Proper physiotherapy, posture correction, rehabilitation exercises, and long-term spinal care are essential for successful recovery.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Designed for advanced motion-preserving spine treatment, artificial disc replacement remains an important innovation in modern spine surgery. Its mobility-preserving capability, precision implant technology, and long-term functional benefits make it a preferred option for selected patients with degenerative disc disease and chronic spinal disorders.</h4><h4><br></h4>",
