                                                    "directionofuse": "<h4>* Undergo complete spine evaluation including MRI, CT scan, X-rays, neurological assessment, and blood tests before surgery.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Inform the healthcare team about all medications, allergies, osteoporosis, diabetes, bleeding disorders, and existing medical conditions.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Follow strict fasting instructions before anesthesia and surgery.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Avoid smoking, alcohol, and blood-thinning medications unless specifically advised by the spine surgeon.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Maintain stable blood pressure, blood sugar, and overall health under medical supervision before surgery.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Follow all hospital hygiene and infection-prevention protocols carefully before and after admission.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Arrange caregiver support and postoperative assistance before discharge.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* After surgery, avoid bending, twisting, heavy lifting, or sudden spine movements during healing.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Do not lift heavy objects or perform high-impact activities until cleared by the spine surgeon.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Wear a brace or spinal support belt if prescribed to stabilize the spine.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Take all prescribed medications exactly as directed, including pain relief, antibiotics, and nerve-related medications if prescribed.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Keep the surgical wound clean, dry, and protected to prevent infection.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Follow physiotherapy and spinal rehabilitation exercises carefully to restore mobility safely.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Maintain correct posture while sitting, standing, and sleeping to protect the artificial disc.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Monitor for warning signs such as severe back/neck pain, numbness, weakness, fever, wound discharge, or loss of bladder/bowel control and report immediately.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Avoid driving, alcohol, and strenuous physical or mental activity until medically cleared.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Attend all follow-up visits and imaging tests to ensure implant stability and proper spinal healing.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>* Follow long-term lifestyle modifications including weight control, posture correction, and core strengthening for spinal health.</h4><h4><br></h4>",
                                                    "precaution": "<p>* Undergo complete spine evaluation including MRI, CT scan, X-rays, neurological assessment, and blood tests before surgery.</p><p><br></p><p>* Inform the surgical team about all medications, allergies, osteoporosis, bleeding disorders, nerve conditions, and existing medical problems.</p><p><br></p><p>* Follow strict fasting instructions before anesthesia and surgery.</p><p><br></p><p>* Avoid smoking, alcohol, and blood-thinning medications unless specifically advised by the spine surgeon.</p><p><br></p><p>* Maintain stable blood pressure, blood sugar, and overall fitness under medical supervision before surgery.</p><p><br></p><p>* Follow all hospital hygiene and infection-prevention protocols carefully before and after admission.</p><p><br></p><p>* Arrange caregiver support and postoperative assistance before discharge.</p><p><br></p><p>* After surgery, avoid bending, twisting, heavy lifting, or sudden spine movements during healing.</p><p><br></p><p>* Do not lift heavy weights or perform high-impact activities until cleared by the spine surgeon.</p><p><br></p><p>* Wear a brace or support belt if prescribed to stabilize the spine during recovery.</p><p><br></p><p>* Take all prescribed medications exactly as directed, including pain relief, antibiotics, and nerve medications if prescribed.</p><p><br></p><p>* Keep the surgical wound clean, dry, and protected to prevent infection.</p><p><br></p><p>* Follow physiotherapy and spinal rehabilitation exercises as instructed to restore mobility safely.</p><p><br></p><p>* Maintain proper posture while sitting, standing, and sleeping to protect the artificial disc.</p><p><br></p><p>* Monitor for warning signs such as severe back pain, leg weakness, numbness, fever, wound discharge, or loss of bladder/bowel control and report immediately.</p><p><br></p><p>* Avoid driving, alcohol, and strenuous physical or mental activity until medically cleared.</p><p><br></p><p>* Attend all follow-up visits and imaging tests to ensure proper implant positioning and spinal healing.</p><p><br></p><p>* Follow long-term lifestyle modifications including weight control, posture correction, and core strengthening for spinal health.</p>",
                                                    "sideeffects": "<p>* Pain, stiffness, or discomfort in the neck or lower back after surgery.</p><p>* Temporary reduced mobility and difficulty bending, twisting, or sitting for long periods.</p><p>* Muscle weakness or fatigue during recovery and rehabilitation.</p><p>* Swelling, bruising, or soreness around the surgical incision site.</p><p>* Numbness, tingling, or nerve irritation in the arms or legs depending on spinal level.</p><p>* Headache or general discomfort in early postoperative recovery.</p><p>* Risk of wound infection or delayed healing at the surgical site.</p><p>* Implant-related complications such as shifting, wear, or improper positioning in rare cases.</p><p>* Persistent or recurrent pain if nerve compression is not fully resolved.</p><p>* Reduced range of motion if scar tissue develops around the spine.</p><p>* Blood clots or vascular complications in rare postoperative cases.</p><p>* Cerebrospinal fluid leak or spinal irritation in rare surgical situations.</p><p>* Anesthesia-related side effects including nausea, dizziness, or fatigue.</p><p>* Emotional stress or frustration due to recovery time and movement restrictions.</p><p>* Long-term follow-up may be required to monitor implant stability and spinal function.</p>",
