                                                    "_id": "699ed1714c30c74154a83111",
                                                    "name": "Endovascular Coiling",
                                                    "description": "<h4>Endovascular Coiling is a minimally invasive neurovascular procedure used to treat brain aneurysms and certain vascular abnormalities. The procedure involves inserting a thin catheter through a blood vessel, usually from the groin or wrist, and guiding it to the site of the aneurysm in the brain or affected vessel. Once positioned, soft platinum coils are released into the aneurysm sac to fill it, promote blood clot formation, and prevent rupture or further bleeding.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>The procedure is typically performed by a neurosurgeon or interventional neuroradiologist in a specialized neurointerventional or stroke center under general anesthesia. Advanced real-time imaging (angiography) is used throughout the procedure to precisely guide the catheter and ensure accurate coil placement while protecting surrounding blood vessels and brain structures.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Modern endovascular coiling procedures are supported by high-resolution digital angiography systems, microcatheter technology, neuro-navigation systems, advanced embolization materials, and multidisciplinary neurovascular teams that improve precision, reduce complications, and enhance patient safety outcomes. In some cases, additional devices like stents or flow diverters may be used to support coil placement.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Endovascular coiling is highly beneficial for preventing aneurysm rupture, reducing the risk of brain hemorrhage, relieving pressure on surrounding brain tissue, and improving long-term neurological outcomes. It is a preferred minimally invasive alternative to open brain surgery for many aneurysm cases due to faster recovery and reduced surgical trauma.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Like all neurovascular procedures, endovascular coiling carries certain risks including bleeding, vessel perforation, stroke, clot formation, coil migration, infection, and recurrence of aneurysm in some cases. There may also be anesthesia-related risks such as nausea, headache, or temporary confusion. Careful follow-up imaging and long-term neurological monitoring are essential for ensuring treatment success and detecting any recurrence early.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Designed for advanced brain aneurysm management and minimally invasive neurovascular care, endovascular coiling remains one of the most important innovations in modern neurosurgery. Its minimally invasive nature, high precision, and strong protective effect against aneurysm rupture make it a preferred treatment option for many cerebrovascular conditions.</h4><h4><br></h4>",
