                                                    "directionofuse": "<h4>* Undergo complete neurovascular evaluation including cerebral angiography, MRI, CT scan, kidney function tests, and blood tests before the procedure.</h4><h4>* Inform the healthcare team about all medications, allergies, bleeding disorders, kidney disease, diabetes, pregnancy status, and existing medical conditions.</h4><h4>* Follow all fasting instructions carefully before anesthesia and the procedure.</h4><h4>* Avoid smoking, alcohol, and blood-thinning medications unless specifically instructed by the specialist.</h4><h4>* Maintain stable blood pressure and overall cardiovascular health under medical supervision before treatment.</h4><h4>* Follow all hospital hygiene and sterile protocols carefully before and after the procedure.</h4><h4>* Arrange caregiver support and post-procedure monitoring before discharge.</h4><h4>* After the procedure, rest and avoid heavy physical activity, sudden movements, or exertion for the recommended recovery period.</h4><h4>* Keep the catheter insertion site (groin or wrist) clean, dry, and protected to prevent infection or bleeding.</h4><h4>* Take all prescribed medications exactly as directed, including blood thinners, pain relief medicines, anti-seizure drugs, or blood pressure medications if prescribed.</h4><h4>* Monitor for severe headache, weakness, vision changes, speech difficulty, confusion, seizure activity, or sudden neurological symptoms and report immediately.</h4><h4>* Watch for swelling, bleeding, or pain at the insertion site.</h4><h4>* Avoid driving, alcohol, and operating machinery until medically cleared.</h4><h4>* Maintain proper hydration, rest, and a calm environment to support recovery.</h4><h4>* Attend all follow-up imaging and neurology appointments to ensure aneurysm closure and long-term stability.</h4><h4>* Follow long-term neurological and vascular care instructions strictly to prevent recurrence and complications.</h4><h4><br></h4>",
                                                    "precaution": "<p>* Undergo complete neurovascular evaluation including cerebral angiography, MRI, CT scan, and blood tests before the procedure.</p><p>* Inform the healthcare team about all medications, allergies, bleeding disorders, kidney problems, pregnancy status, and existing medical conditions.</p><p>* Follow all fasting instructions carefully before anesthesia and the procedure.</p><p>* Avoid smoking, alcohol, and blood-thinning medications unless specifically instructed by the specialist.</p><p>* Maintain stable blood pressure and overall cardiovascular health under medical supervision before treatment.</p><p>* Follow all hospital hygiene and sterile protocols carefully before and after the procedure.</p><p>* Arrange caregiver support and post-procedure monitoring before discharge.</p><p>* After the procedure, avoid heavy lifting, sudden movements, or strenuous physical activity during recovery.</p><p>* Keep the catheter insertion site (groin or wrist) clean, dry, and protected to prevent infection or bleeding.</p><p>* Take all prescribed medications exactly as directed, including blood thinners, pain relief medicines, and anti-seizure or blood pressure medications if prescribed.</p><p>* Monitor for sudden headache, weakness, vision changes, speech difficulty, confusion, or seizure activity and report immediately.</p><p>* Watch for signs of bleeding, swelling, or pain at the insertion site.</p><p>* Avoid driving or operating heavy machinery until medically cleared.</p><p>* Maintain proper hydration, rest, and a calm environment to support recovery.</p><p>* Attend all follow-up imaging and neurology appointments to ensure aneurysm closure and detect recurrence early.</p><p>* Follow long-term neurological and vascular care instructions strictly for optimal outcomes.</p>",
                                                    "sideeffects": "<p>* Headache or head pressure after the procedure.</p><p>* Pain, bruising, or swelling at the catheter insertion site (groin or wrist).</p><p>* Fatigue, dizziness, or mild confusion during early recovery.</p><p>* Nausea or vomiting due to anesthesia or vascular manipulation.</p><p>* Temporary weakness, numbness, or neurological changes depending on vessel involvement.</p><p>* Risk of bleeding or hematoma at the insertion site.</p><p>* Blood vessel injury or vessel spasm during catheter navigation.</p><p>* Blood clot formation (thrombosis) or stroke in rare cases.</p><p>* Coil migration or incomplete aneurysm occlusion requiring further treatment.</p><p>* Recurrence or reopening of the aneurysm over time.</p><p>* Infection at the access site or rarely within treated vessels.</p><p>* Allergic reaction to contrast dye used in imaging.</p><p>* Kidney strain or contrast-induced complications in susceptible patients.</p><p>* Seizures or neurological symptoms in rare brain-related cases.</p><p>* Emotional stress or anxiety during recovery and follow-up monitoring.</p><p>* Long-term follow-up imaging may be required to ensure aneurysm stability and treatment success.</p>",
