                                                    "_id": "699ed5ba27510d3ddf6c809d",
                                                    "name": "Total Knee Replacement",
                                                    "description": "<h4>Total Knee Replacement is a major orthopedic surgical procedure performed to replace the entire damaged knee joint with artificial prosthetic components. The procedure is commonly recommended for severe knee arthritis, advanced degenerative joint disease, rheumatoid arthritis, post-traumatic arthritis, deformity of the knee, or chronic knee pain that significantly affects walking, mobility, and daily activities. The surgery aims to relieve pain, restore joint function, improve stability, and enhance overall quality of life.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>The procedure is usually performed by an orthopedic joint replacement surgeon or knee specialist under general or regional anesthesia in a hospital or specialized orthopedic surgical center. During the surgery, the damaged bone and cartilage from the femur, tibia, and sometimes the patella are removed and replaced with prosthetic components made of metal and medical-grade plastic designed to replicate smooth joint movement and durability.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Modern total knee replacement procedures are supported by advanced imaging systems, robotic-assisted surgical platforms, computer-guided alignment technologies, precision orthopedic instruments, custom implant systems, minimally invasive surgical techniques in selected cases, and multidisciplinary rehabilitation programs that improve surgical accuracy, reduce complications, minimize tissue trauma, and support faster recovery outcomes.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Total knee replacement is highly beneficial for reducing chronic knee pain, improving walking ability, restoring mobility and flexibility, improving joint stability, correcting deformity, enhancing independence, improving sleep quality, and improving long-term musculoskeletal health and quality of life. Early diagnosis and timely surgical treatment significantly improve functional recovery and joint preservation outcomes.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Like all major orthopedic surgical procedures, total knee replacement carries certain risks including infection, bleeding, blood clots, implant loosening, joint stiffness, swelling, nerve injury, prosthetic wear, scar tissue formation, anesthesia-related complications, prolonged rehabilitation, and incomplete recovery of strength or mobility in some cases. Proper physiotherapy, rehabilitation exercises, knee protection, nutritional support, and long-term orthopedic follow-up care are essential for successful implant function and recovery.</h4><h4><br></h4><h4>Designed for advanced knee reconstruction and joint restoration, total knee replacement remains one of the most successful and widely performed procedures in orthopedic surgery. Its pain-relief capability, precision implant technologies, advanced reconstructive techniques, and long-term functional recovery benefits make total knee replacement a preferred treatment option for patients with severe knee joint disorders and advanced degeneration.</h4><h4><br></h4>",
