                                                    "directionofuse": "<h4>* Undergo complete orthopedic evaluation including X-rays, MRI, blood tests, knee alignment assessment, and anesthesia evaluation before surgery.</h4><h4>* Inform the healthcare team about all medications, allergies, previous knee surgeries, arthritis history, bleeding disorders, and existing medical conditions.</h4><h4>* Follow all fasting instructions carefully before anesthesia and surgery.</h4><h4>* Avoid smoking, alcohol, blood-thinning medications, and certain supplements before surgery as advised by the healthcare provider.</h4><h4>* Maintain proper hydration, balanced nutrition, and infection prevention measures under medical supervision before the procedure.</h4><h4>* Follow all hospital hygiene and sterile care protocols carefully before and after surgery.</h4><h4>* Arrange transportation, mobility assistance, home support, and caregiver care before hospital discharge.</h4><h4>* After surgery, avoid high-impact activities such as running, jumping, squatting, kneeling, or sudden twisting movements during healing.</h4><h4>* Use walkers, crutches, braces, compression garments, or knee support devices exactly as prescribed by the orthopedic specialist.</h4><h4>* Follow physiotherapy and rehabilitation programs carefully to restore knee strength, flexibility, balance, and movement safely.</h4><h4>* Keep surgical wounds clean, dry, and protected throughout the recovery period.</h4><h4>* Take prescribed pain medications, antibiotics, blood clot prevention medicines, anti-inflammatory medicines, and post-operative treatments exactly as directed.</h4><h4>* Maintain proper hydration, protein-rich nutrition, calcium support, and adequate rest to promote bone healing and implant stability.</h4><h4>* Monitor for fever, severe pain, swelling, redness, wound discharge, knee instability, calf pain, breathing difficulty, or signs of infection and report them immediately to healthcare professionals.</h4><h4>* Attend all follow-up appointments for implant evaluation, imaging review, knee function assessment, and rehabilitation monitoring.</h4><h4>* Follow activity restrictions carefully until complete recovery and medical clearance are achieved to prevent implant loosening or knee re-injury.</h4><h4><br></h4>",
                                                    "precaution": "<p>* Undergo complete orthopedic evaluation including X-rays, MRI, blood tests, knee alignment assessment, and anesthesia evaluation before surgery.</p><p>* Inform the healthcare team about all medications, allergies, previous knee surgeries, arthritis history, bleeding disorders, and existing medical conditions.</p><p>* Follow all fasting instructions carefully before anesthesia and surgery.</p><p>* Avoid smoking, alcohol, blood-thinning medications, and certain supplements before surgery as advised by the healthcare provider.</p><p>* Maintain proper hydration, balanced nutrition, and infection prevention measures under medical supervision before the procedure.</p><p>* Follow all hospital hygiene and sterile care protocols carefully before and after surgery.</p><p>* Arrange transportation, mobility assistance, home support, and caregiver care before hospital discharge.</p><p>* After surgery, avoid high-impact activities such as running, jumping, squatting, kneeling, or sudden twisting movements during healing.</p><p>* Use walkers, crutches, braces, compression garments, or knee support devices exactly as prescribed by the orthopedic specialist.</p><p>* Follow physiotherapy and rehabilitation programs carefully to restore knee strength, flexibility, balance, and movement safely.</p><p>* Keep surgical wounds clean, dry, and protected throughout the recovery period.</p><p>* Take prescribed pain medications, antibiotics, blood clot prevention medicines, anti-inflammatory medicines, and post-operative treatments exactly as directed.</p><p>* Maintain proper hydration, protein-rich nutrition, calcium support, and adequate rest to promote bone healing and implant stability.</p><p>* Monitor for fever, severe pain, swelling, redness, wound discharge, knee instability, calf pain, breathing difficulty, or signs of infection and report them immediately to healthcare professionals.</p><p>* Attend all follow-up appointments for implant evaluation, imaging review, knee function assessment, and rehabilitation monitoring.</p><p>* Follow activity restrictions carefully until complete recovery and medical clearance are achieved to prevent implant loosening or knee re-injury.</p>",
                                                    "sideeffects": "<p>* Pain, swelling, tenderness, or stiffness around the knee after surgery.</p><p>* Temporary reduced mobility and difficulty walking during early recovery.</p><p>* Bruising, redness, or mild bleeding around the surgical incision site.</p><p>* Muscle weakness or reduced strength in the leg during rehabilitation.</p><p>* Difficulty climbing stairs, bending the knee, or performing daily activities during recovery.</p><p>* Temporary numbness, tingling, or nerve irritation around the knee or lower leg.</p><p>* Fatigue or reduced energy levels during healing and physiotherapy.</p><p>* Risk of wound infection, delayed healing, or inflammation around the joint.</p><p>* Scar tissue formation leading to stiffness or reduced knee flexibility.</p><p>* Temporary dependence on walkers, crutches, or knee braces during recovery.</p><p>* Rare complications such as blood clots, implant loosening, joint instability, prosthetic wear, persistent pain, or reduced range of motion.</p><p>* Anesthesia-related side effects including nausea, dizziness, headache, or fatigue.</p><p>* Emotional stress or frustration related to long rehabilitation and restricted movement.</p><p>* Muscle imbalance or gait changes during early recovery stages.</p><p>* Long-term orthopedic follow-up and physiotherapy may be required to monitor implant stability, knee function, and overall recovery outcomes.</p>",
