                                                    "_id": "69b79e992ca87bff88a95bb6",
                                                    "name": "Renal calculi surgery",
                                                    "description": "<p>Renal calculi surgery is a specialized urological procedure performed to remove kidney stones or urinary tract stones that are too large, painful, obstructive, infected, or difficult to pass naturally through the urinary system. Kidney stones are hard mineral and salt deposits that form inside the kidneys and may cause severe pain, urinary blockage, bleeding, infection, nausea, and kidney damage if left untreated. Surgical treatment is commonly recommended when medications, hydration, or non-invasive treatments fail to relieve the condition.</p><p><br></p><p>The procedure is usually performed by a urologist in a hospital or specialized urology center under general, spinal, or local anesthesia depending on the type and complexity of the stone. Several surgical techniques may be used including ureteroscopy (URS), laser lithotripsy, percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL), extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy (ESWL), or open/laparoscopic stone removal in rare complex cases. The selected technique depends on the size, location, number, and composition of the kidney stones.</p><p><br></p><p>Modern kidney stone surgeries are supported by advanced endoscopic instruments, laser stone fragmentation systems, fluoroscopic imaging guidance, ultrasound technology, minimally invasive surgical techniques, and precision urological equipment that improve stone removal accuracy, reduce tissue damage, minimize pain, and support faster recovery. Many procedures involve inserting thin scopes through the urinary tract to locate and break stones into smaller fragments for removal or natural passage.</p><p><br></p><p>Kidney stone surgery is highly beneficial for relieving severe pain, restoring urine flow, removing urinary obstruction, preventing kidney damage, reducing recurrent infections, and improving overall urinary system health. The procedure helps patients regain comfort, normal kidney function, and better quality of life while reducing complications associated with untreated urinary stones.</p><p><br></p><p>Like all surgical procedures, kidney stone surgery carries certain risks including bleeding, infection, pain during urination, temporary blood in urine, swelling, ureteral injury, residual stone fragments, urinary discomfort, anesthesia-related complications, and recurrence of kidney stones. Proper hydration, medication management, dietary modifications, follow-up imaging, and long-term kidney care significantly improve recovery and reduce recurrence risk.</p><p><br></p><p>Designed for advanced urinary stone management and minimally invasive urological care, kidney stone surgery remains one of the most effective and commonly performed procedures in modern urology. Its precision techniques, advanced laser technology, minimally invasive approaches, and improved recovery outcomes make kidney stone surgery a preferred treatment option for patients suffering from painful or complicated urinary stone disease.</p>",
