                                                    "directionofuse": "<p>* Undergo complete medical evaluation including thyroid function tests, blood tests, ultrasound, biopsy, ECG, and imaging studies before surgery.</p><p>* Inform the healthcare team about all medications, allergies, thyroid disorders, bleeding conditions, and previous surgeries.</p><p>* Stop blood-thinning medications, smoking, alcohol, and certain supplements before surgery according to medical advice.</p><p>* Follow fasting instructions carefully before anesthesia and the surgical procedure.</p><p>* Patients with hyperthyroidism may require medication to stabilize thyroid hormone levels before surgery.</p><p>* Maintain proper hydration and balanced nutrition before the procedure unless otherwise instructed.</p><p>* Arrange transportation and caregiver support before hospital admission and discharge.</p><p>* Follow all pre-operative hygiene and hospital preparation guidelines carefully.</p><p>* After surgery, take prescribed pain medications, antibiotics, calcium supplements, and thyroid hormone replacement therapy exactly as directed.</p><p>* Keep the surgical incision clean, dry, and protected during the healing period.</p><p>* Avoid excessive neck movement, heavy lifting, strenuous exercise, and physically demanding activities during recovery.</p><p>* Sleep with the head slightly elevated if advised to reduce swelling and improve comfort.</p><p>* Follow a soft and comfortable diet initially if swallowing discomfort occurs after surgery.</p><p>* Drink adequate fluids and maintain proper nutrition to support healing and recovery.</p><p>* Monitor for signs of infection, swelling, bleeding, breathing difficulty, numbness, tingling, hoarseness, or severe pain and report them immediately to healthcare professionals.</p><p>* Attend all follow-up appointments for wound evaluation, thyroid hormone monitoring, calcium assessment, and recovery management.</p><p>* Protect the neck incision from direct sunlight, contamination, or physical irritation during healing.</p><p>* Gradually return to normal activities only after medical clearance from the surgeon.</p><p>* Continue long-term thyroid hormone monitoring and endocrine follow-up care if total thyroidectomy was performed.</p>",
                                                    "precaution": "<p>* Undergo complete medical evaluation, thyroid function tests, imaging scans, and pre-surgical investigations before the procedure.</p><p>* Inform the surgeon about all medications, allergies, thyroid disorders, bleeding problems, and previous surgeries before treatment.</p><p>* Avoid blood-thinning medications, smoking, alcohol, and certain supplements before surgery as advised by the healthcare provider.</p><p>* Follow fasting instructions carefully before anesthesia and surgery.</p><p>* Patients with hyperthyroidism may require thyroid hormone control and stabilization before the procedure.</p><p>* Arrange proper post-surgical support and transportation after hospital discharge.</p><p>* Maintain proper hydration and nutritional intake before surgery according to medical guidance.</p><p>* Follow all hospital infection prevention and pre-operative preparation instructions carefully.</p><p>* After surgery, avoid excessive neck movement, heavy lifting, strenuous activities, and physical strain during recovery.</p><p>* Monitor the surgical site regularly for swelling, redness, bleeding, discharge, or signs of infection.</p><p>* Report symptoms such as breathing difficulty, severe neck swelling, hoarseness, numbness, tingling, or difficulty swallowing immediately to healthcare professionals.</p><p>* Take prescribed medications, pain relievers, calcium supplements, and thyroid hormone replacement therapy exactly as directed.</p><p>* Attend all follow-up appointments for wound evaluation, thyroid monitoring, and recovery assessment.</p><p>* Protect the surgical incision area from excessive sunlight, contamination, or physical injury during healing.</p><p>* Follow long-term thyroid hormone monitoring and endocrine care recommendations after total thyroidectomy if required.</p>",
                                                    "sideeffects": "<p>* Pain, swelling, or discomfort around the neck incision area after surgery.</p><p>* Temporary sore throat, difficulty swallowing, or mild neck stiffness during recovery.</p><p>* Hoarseness or temporary voice changes caused by irritation near the vocal cord nerves.</p><p>* Fatigue, weakness, or reduced energy levels during the healing process.</p><p>* Mild bruising or scarring around the surgical site.</p><p>* Temporary low calcium levels (hypocalcemia) which may cause tingling, numbness, or muscle cramps.</p><p>* Hormonal imbalance or hypothyroidism after partial or total thyroid removal.</p><p>* Need for lifelong thyroid hormone replacement therapy after total thyroidectomy.</p><p>* Temporary breathing discomfort or throat tightness in some patients after surgery.</p><p>* Risk of wound infection, redness, or delayed healing around the incision site.</p><p>* Bleeding or fluid collection near the surgical area in rare cases.</p><p>* Temporary nausea, dizziness, or anesthesia-related side effects after the procedure.</p><p>* Emotional stress, mood changes, or sleep disturbances during hormonal adjustment.</p><p>* Rare risk of permanent vocal cord nerve injury leading to persistent voice changes.</p><p>* Rare complications involving parathyroid gland damage affecting calcium regulation.</p>",
